== The Epistle Dedicatory

Beloved in our dearest Lord,

Christ, the Scripture, your#super[+] own hearts, and Satan’s devices are the four prime things that should be first and most studied and searched. If any cast off the study of these, they cannot be safe here, nor happy#footnote[If a minister had as many eyes as Argus to watch, as many heads as Typhoeus to plan, and as many hands as Briareus to labor, he might find work enough for them all.] hereafter. It is my work as a Christian, but much more as I am a #emph[watchman,] to do my best to reveal the fullness of Christ, the emptiness of created things, and the snares of the great deceiver; and this I have tried to do (in the following discourse) according to that measure of grace which I have received from the Lord. God once accepted a handful of flour for a sacrifice, and a fistful of goats’ hair for an offering; and I know that you#super[+] have not so learned the Father as to despise the day of small things. Beloved, Satan, having fallen from light to darkness, from happiness to misery, from heaven to hell, from an angel to a devil, is so full of malice and envy that he will leave no means untried by which he may make all others eternally miserable with himself. Being shut out of heaven, #emph[and shut up under chains of darkness until the judgment of the great day,] he uses all his power and skill to bring all the sons of men into the same condition and condemnation with himself. Satan has cast such sinful seed into our souls that now he no sooner tempts than we are ready to assent; he no sooner has a plot against us than he makes a conquest of us. If he only shows men a little of the beauty and splendor of the world, how ready they are to fall down and worship him!

Whatever sin the heart of man is most prone to, the devil will help it forward. If David is proud of#footnote[2 Sam 24.] his people, Satan will provoke him to number them, so that he may be prouder still. If Peter is slavishly#footnote[Matt 16:22; 26:69 to the end of the chapter.] fearful, Satan will drive him to rebuke and deny Christ, to save his own skin. If Ahab’s#footnote[1 Kgs 22.] prophets are given to flattery, the devil will at once #emph[become a lying spirit in the mouths of four hundred of them,] and they will flatter Ahab to his ruin. If Judas will#footnote[John 13:2.] be a traitor, Satan will quickly enter into his heart and make him sell his master for money, which some heathens would never have done. If Ananias will lie#footnote[Acts 5:3.] for gain, #emph[Satan will fill his heart so that he may lie] (with a vengeance) #emph[to the Holy Spirit.] Satan loves to sail with the wind, and to suit men’s temptations to their conditions and inclinations. If they are in prosperity, he will tempt them to deny God; if they are in adversity, he will tempt them to distrust#footnote[Prov 30:9.] God. If their knowledge is weak, he will tempt them to have low thoughts of God; if their conscience is tender, he will tempt them to over-scrupulousness; if it is lax, to carnal complacency. If we are bold-spirited, he will tempt us to presumption; if timid, to despair; if pliable, to inconstancy; if stiff, to impenitence, etc.

From the power, malice, and skill of Satan proceed all the soul-killing plots, devices, stratagems, and machinations that are in the world. He has various devices to draw souls to sin, and various plots to keep souls from all holy and heavenly services, and various stratagems to keep souls in a mourning, staggering, doubting, and questioning condition. He has various devices to destroy the great and honorable, the wise and learned, the blind and ignorant, the rich and the poor, the real and the nominal saints, etc. At one time he will refrain from tempting, so that we may think ourselves complacent and neglect our watch; at another he will seem to flee, so that he may make us proud of the victory. At one time he will fix men’s eyes more on others’ sins than on their own, so that he may puff them up; at another he will fix their eyes more on others’ graces than on their own, so that he may overwhelm them, etc. A man may as well count the stars and number the sands of the sea as reckon up all the devices of Satan. Yet those which are most important, and by which he does most harm to the precious souls of men, are uncovered in the following treatise, and the #emph[remedies] against them prescribed, etc.

Beloved, I think it necessary to give you#super[+] and the world a faithful account of the reasons moving me to appear in print in these days, in which we may say there was never more writing and yet never less practicing; and they are as follows, etc.

First, because Satan has a greater influence on men, and higher advantages over men (having, as it were, the wind and the hill), than they think he has; and the knowledge of his high advantages is the highway to frustrating him, and to making the soul strong in resisting and happy in conquering, etc.

Your#super[+] urgent pleading, and the urgent pleading of many other precious sons of Zion, has, after much striving with God, my own heart, and others, made a conquest of me, and forced me to do at last what at first was not a little contrary to my inclination and resolution, etc.

The strange opposition that I#footnote[Pirates make the strongest and fiercest attacks on those vessels that are most richly laden; so does Satan, that arch-pirate, against those truths that have most of God, Christ, and heaven in them.] met with from Satan in the writing of this following discourse has put an edge on my spirit, for I know that Satan strives mightily to keep from the light those things that tend most of all to shake and break his kingdom of darkness, and to lift up the kingdom and glory of the Lord Jesus Christ in the souls and lives of the children of men, etc.

Its exceeding usefulness to all sorts, ranks, and conditions of men in the world. Here you#super[+] have a salve for every sore, and a plaster for every wound, and a #emph[remedy] against every disease, especially against those that tend most to the undoing of souls and to the ruin of the state, etc.

I do not know of anyone who has written on this subject; all that I have ever seen have only touched on this string. This has been no small provocation to me to attempt something in this way, so that others who have better heads and hearts may be the more stirred to make use of their talents in a further uncovering of Satan’s devices, and in making known such choice #emph[remedies] as may enable the souls of men to triumph over all his plots and stratagems, etc.

I have many precious friends in various parts of the country who are not a little desirous that my pen may reach them, now that my voice cannot. By the help of the mighty God of Jacob I have formerly been a weak instrument of good to them, and I cannot but hope and believe that the Lord will also bless these labors to them, since they are (in part) the fruit of their desires and prayers, etc.

Lastly, not knowing how soon my hourglass may run out, and how soon I may be cut off by the hand of death from all opportunities of doing further service for Christ or your#super[+] souls in this world, I was willing to sow a little handful of spiritual seed among you#super[+], so that when I lay aside this earthly tabernacle, my love to you#super[+], and that dear remembrance of you#super[+] which I have in my soul, may strongly move your#super[+] minds and spirits to make this book your#super[+] companion, and under all outward or inward changes to make use of this heavenly salve. I hope it will, by the blessing of the Lord, be as effectual for the healing of all your#super[+] wounds as looking up to the bronze serpent was effectual to heal those who were bitten and stung by fiery serpents. I will leave this book with you#super[+] as a legacy of my dearest love, desiring the Lord to make it a far greater and sweeter legacy than all those carnal legacies that are left by the high and mighty ones of the earth to their nearest and dearest relations, etc.

Beloved, I would not have affection carry my pen too far beyond my intention; therefore allow me only to express my desires for you#super[+] and my desires to you#super[+], and I will draw to a close.

My desires for you#super[+] are “that out of the riches#footnote[Eph 3.] of His glory He may strengthen you#super[+] with power through His Spirit in your#super[+] inner#footnote[Verse 17.] being, so that Christ may dwell in your#super[+] hearts through faith; that you#super[+], being rooted and grounded in love, may#footnote[Verse 18.] have power, together with all the saints, to comprehend the length and width and height and depth#footnote[Verse 19.] of the love of Christ, and to know this love that surpasses knowledge; that you#super[+] may be filled with all the fullness of God.” And “that you#super[+] may walk#footnote[Col 1:10.] in a manner worthy of the Lord and may please Him in every way, bearing fruit in every good work, growing in the knowledge of God, being strengthened with all power according#footnote[Verse 11.] to His glorious might, so that you#super[+] may have full endurance and patience with joy.” “That you#super[+] will not do anything wrong.” “That#footnote[2 Cor 13:7.] your#super[+] love may abound more and more in knowledge and depth of insight,#footnote[Phil 1:9.] so that you#super[+] may be able to test and prove what is best, and may be pure#footnote[Verse 10.] and blameless for the day of Christ.” And “that our God will#footnote[2 Thess 1:11.] count you#super[+] worthy of His calling, and will fulfill every good desire and work of faith with#footnote[Verse 12.] power, so that the name of our Lord Jesus may be glorified in you#super[+], and you#super[+] in Him, according to the grace of our God and of the Lord Jesus Christ.” And that you#super[+] may be eminent in#footnote[Ps 93:5.] holiness, holiness being Zion’s glory; that your#super[+] hearts may be kept upright, your#super[+] judgments sound, and your#super[+] lives blameless. That as you#super[+] are now my joy, so in the day of Christ you#super[+] may be my crown. That I may see my labors in your#super[+] lives; that your#super[+] conduct may not be earthly when the things you#super[+] hear are heavenly, but that it may be worthy of the gospel. That as the fish that live in the salt sea are yet fresh, so you#super[+], though you#super[+] live in an uncharitable world, may yet be charitable and loving. That you#super[+] may, like the bee, suck honey out of every flower. That you#super[+] may shine in a sea of troubles, as the pearl shines in the sky, though it grows in the sea. That in all your#super[+] trials you#super[+] may be like the stone in Thrace that neither burns in the fire nor sinks in the water. That you#super[+] may be like the heavens, excellent in substance and beautiful in appearance, so that you#super[+] may meet me with joy on that day when Christ will say to his Father, “Here am I, and the children You have given Me.”

My desires to you#super[+] are that you#super[+] would make it your#super[+] business to study Christ, his Word, your#super[+] own hearts, Satan’s plots, and eternity more than ever. That you#super[+] would strive more to be inwardly sincere than outwardly glorious; to live, rather than to have a name for being alive. That you#super[+] would labor with all your#super[+] might to be thankful under blessings, and faithful in your#super[+] places, and humble under divine appearances, and fruitful under precious ordinances; that as your#super[+] means and blessings are greater than others’, so your#super[+] account before God may not prove worse than others’. That you#super[+] would pray for me, who am not worthy to be named among the saints, that I may be a precious instrument in the hand of Christ to bring in many souls to him, and to build up those who are brought in, in their most holy faith; and that utterance may be given to me, that I may make known all the will of God. That#footnote[2 Cor 11:23, 24, 25, 26, 27, 28.] I may be sincere, faithful, frequent, fervent, and constant in the work of the Lord, and that my labor may not be in vain in the Lord; that my labors may be accepted by the Lord and his saints, and that I may daily see the travail of my soul, etc.

But above all, pray for me, that I may more and more find the power and sweetness of those things on my own heart that I give out to you#super[+] and others; and that my soul may be so visited with strength from on high that I may live fully and constantly up to those truths that I hold out to the world; and that I may be, both in life and doctrine, a burning and a shining light; so that when the Lord Jesus appears, I may receive a crown of glory, which he will give to me on that day, and not only to me, but to all who love his appearing, etc.

To close, remember this: your#super[+] life is short, your#super[+] duties many, your#super[+] help great, and your#super[+] reward sure. Therefore do not lose heart; hold on, and hold up in ways of well-doing, and heaven will make amends for all.

I will now take leave of you#super[+], now that my heart has, by my hand, signed that I am,

#v(1em)

#align(right)[Your#super[+] loving pastor under Christ, in all pastoral affection and commitment, in our dearest Lord.]

#align(right)[THOMAS BROOKS.]
